        "id": "fd_0245",
        "title": "Prime-Field Double Descent Controlled by Modular Representation Type",
        "description": "Conjecture: For families of neural networks trained exactly on arithmetic tasks over finite fields F_p, the presence or absence of epoch-wise double descent in test error is determined by the representation type of the task algebra over F_p: tasks whose induced module category is tame exhibit a bounded number of descent peaks uniformly in p, while wild-type tasks exhibit an unbounded number of peaks along an infinite subsequence of primes. Test: Define a concrete family of tasks (e.g. modular polynomial evaluation, inversion, and bilinear map learning), associate to each task a finite-dimensional algebra capturing its compositional symmetries, classify the algebra as finite/tame/wild for each p, and train matched architectures across many primes while counting statistically robust test-error peaks over training time. The conjecture is refuted if wild-type tasks fail to generate growing peak counts or tame-type tasks show unbounded peak proliferation under controlled scaling. Impact: This would connect modular representation theory to learning dynamics, giving a structural predictor of grokking and double-descent phenomena and suggesting a new algebraic taxonomy of trainability.",
