        "id": "fd_0345",
        "title": "p-adic Newton Fracture Threshold for Polynomial Optimization",
        "description": "Conjecture: For every degree-d polynomial f in Z[x_1,...,x_n] with isolated nondegenerate critical points modulo p, there exists a prime-dependent threshold exponent e_0 = O_d(log H(f)) such that for all e >= e_0, the directed graph of Newton iterations on (Z/p^e Z)^n undergoes a sharp structural transition: each residue class lying in the Hensel basin of a mod-p critical point reaches a unique lifted critical point in O(e) steps, while outside the union of these basins the proportion of starting points entering cycles of length > 1 is bounded below by a constant c(d,n,p) > 0. Test: Compute Newton-iteration graphs for random polynomial families over Z/p^e Z as e grows; confirm/refute by measuring (1) linear-in-e convergence inside predicted Hensel basins and (2) nonvanishing density of nontrivial cycles outside them. A proof would require combining p-adic dynamical systems, Hensel lifting, and arithmetic singularity analysis. Impact: This would reveal a previously unmodeled arithmetic phase transition in discrete optimization, giving a rigorous basin/cycle decomposition for p-adic iterative methods and suggesting new optimization algorithms and hardness diagnostics over finite precision arithmetic.",
