        "description": "Conjecture: In a sufficiently large formal mathematics library, the directed dependency graph of theorem proofs admits a nontrivial coarse-graining map into finitely many 'proof universality classes' such that, after quotienting by these classes, the distribution of minimal proof lengths and tactic-sequence motifs is asymptotically invariant under library growth. Test: Extract dependency/tactic graphs from successive snapshots of a large corpus (e.g. Lean/mathlib), cluster proofs by normalized local rewrite/inference statistics, and check whether the induced class distribution and inter-class transition matrix stabilize as the library expands; refute if no coarse-graining yields stable statistics beyond finite-size effects. Impact: Would introduce a statistical-mechanics style structure theory for formal mathematics, enabling transfer learning across theorem domains, principled benchmark generation, and potentially new lower/upper-bound heuristics for automated proof search.",
