        "title": "Resolution Refutations Have a Persistent Homology Fingerprint Predicting Clause ",
        "description": "Conjecture: There exists a canonical construction sending any unsatisfiable CNF formula F to a filtered simplicial complex K_t(F), built from clause-variable incidence neighborhoods at scale t, such that the maximum persistence of 1-dimensional homology in K_t(F) is polynomially related to the minimum clause space of F for broad natural families (including pebbling and Tseitin formulas). Test: Define the construction explicitly, compute persistent H1 for benchmark families, and check whether formulas with provably high clause space also exhibit provably large H1 persistence while low-space families do not; refutation occurs if no such correlation survives across these standard families. Impact: This would create a new topological invariant for proof complexity, potentially turning space lower bounds into computable TDA-style obstructions and linking SAT solving, topology, and proof complexity in a genuinely new way.",
