        "id": "fd_0387",
        "title": "Proof-Search Curvature Predicts Transfer Across Formal Theories",
        "description": "Conjecture: Let T1 and T2 be two formal theorem libraries, and let G1, G2 be theorem-dependency graphs enriched with semantic embeddings of statements. Define the Ollivier-Ricci curvature distribution of each graph using embedding-induced edge weights. Then the zero-shot transfer success of a proof-guidance model trained on T1 and evaluated on T2 is bounded below by an explicit monotone function of the overlap between the low-curvature tails of G1 and G2, and this lower bound is asymptotically stronger than any bound using only degree distribution, spectral gap, or clustering coefficient. Test: compute these graph-curvature statistics on multiple pairs of libraries (e.g. algebra to algebra, algebra to analysis, Lean mathlib subdomains), train identical proof-guidance models, and check whether transfer accuracy is significantly better predicted by low-curvature-tail overlap than by standard graph statistics; refute by exhibiting a baseline statistic with equal or better predictive power across the benchmark suite. Impact: would give a geometric law for when mathematical knowledge transfers between domains, enabling principled curriculum design, theorem-library modularization, and architecture choices for autonomous theorem provers.",
