        "description": "Conjecture: For families of finite proof-search or rewrite-state graphs G_R obtained by truncating search at resource bound R, there exists a computable cohomological observable \\(\\theta_R\\) built from persistent 1-cocycles on the directed flag complex of G_R such that a sharp change in \\(\\theta_R\\) occurs iff the system crosses from predominantly reversible local exploration to globally normalization-dominated dynamics; concretely, in terminating confluent systems the lifetime-weighted rank of non-exact persistent 1-cocycles drops to 0 beyond a critical scale, while in non-confluent or metastable systems it remains bounded away from 0 along an infinite subsequence of R. Test: Construct proof-state/rewrite graphs for benchmark systems (lambda calculus fragments, Knuth-Bendix systems, SAT configuration graphs), compute directed persistent cohomology across increasing R, and check whether cocycle persistence vanishes precisely in systems known to normalize/converge and persists in systems with branching/metastability/non-confluence. A single robust counterexample\u2014persistent cocycles surviving arbitrarily far in a strongly normalizing confluent family, or uniformly vanishing in a provably metastable non-confluent family\u2014refutes the conjecture. Impact: This would introduce a new topological order parameter for theorem proving and symbolic computation, giving a measurable predictor of when search spaces become intrinsically one-way, and could guide restart policies, completion strategies, and normalization heuristics.",
