        "id": "fd_0486",
        "title": "Profinite Limit Law for Persistent Homology Torsion",
        "description": "Conjecture: Let (X_n) be any Benjamini\u2013Schramm convergent sequence of finite bounded-degree simplicial complexes with uniformly bounded dimension. For each homological degree k, the normalized p-primary torsion barcode measure of H_k(X_n; Z), viewed as a measure on birth-death pairs with weights log_p of invariant-factor multiplicities, converges for every prime p to a deterministic limit measure depending only on the local weak limit of (X_n). Moreover, the family of limit measures over all p determines the asymptotic normalized integral barcode up to vanishing error. Test: Compute torsion barcodes for large random complexes and deterministic expander-based complexes with the same local weak limit; confirm convergence and universality across models, or refute by exhibiting two sequences with identical local weak limit but distinct torsion limit laws. Impact: This would create a new local-to-global principle for arithmetic topology, linking random geometry, profinite invariants, and persistent homology, and would enable prediction of large-scale torsion structure from local statistics alone.",
